+# keystone-inventory
+
+[Back to Documentation Index](./README.md)
+
+The basic inventory mechanism — holder-identified menus, one click/drag/close/quit listener, slot click routing,
+plain components, open/close tracking, panel flows, page arithmetic — reconciled from Oriel's and Gangland's
+independent copies in Keystone Phase E6 (`docs/phase-e6-inventory.md`). Each consumer plugin constructs its own
+`InventoryService`; there is no static registry, matching the shared-classloader constraint every Keystone module
+follows.
+
+**Layering.** This module is the floor, not the whole building. Oriel builds its advanced menu product on top of
+it (animation, requirements, argument bindings, provider/filter/sort/search registries, deposit slots, non-chest
+menu types, YAML loaders, an editor) — none of that lives here. If you find yourself wanting to add a feature that
+knows about a specific product's menu *content*, it belongs in your own consumer code (or in Oriel), not here.
+
+Depends on `keystone-common`, `keystone-bean`, `keystone-item`. Shaded into `Keystone.jar` by `keystone-plugin`.
+
+## Usage
+
+```java
+CooldownService cooldowns = ...; // your consumer's CooldownService bean
+InventoryService inventory = new InventoryService(cooldowns); // constructed by the consumer, e.g. a @Bean method
+inventory.registerListeners(plugin); // once, at bootstrap — registers MenuListener under your plugin
+
+ChestMenu shop = ChestMenu.builder(inventory)
+    .title("&6Shop")
+    .rows(6)
+    .border(Material.BLACK_STAINED_GLASS_PANE)
+    .slot(13, ItemComponent.of(Material.DIAMOND)
+        .name("&bBuy Diamond")
+        .lore("&7Click to buy", "&7Costs &e$500")
+        .onLeftClick(ctx -> ctx.player().performCommand("buy diamond")))
+    .build();
+shop.open(player);
+```
+
+`InventoryService`'s reload/shutdown `BeanLifecycle` hooks (`onPreClear`/`onShutdown`) close every menu this
+service is tracking — including one sitting in the vetoed-close reopen window (§ Item-return contract) — with
+`CloseReason.RELOAD`/`CloseReason.PLUGIN_DISABLE`. Nothing extra to wire up.
+
+## The two-service rule
+
+The shared classloader means every consumer's `InventoryService`/`MenuListener` sees every click, drag, close and
+quit event on the server — Oriel's menus and Gangland's alike. Identity is
+`event.getInventory().getHolder() instanceof MenuHolder holder && holder.service() == this.service`: each
+`MenuHolder` carries the exact `InventoryService` instance that built it, so a click on Oriel's menu never reaches
+Gangland's `MenuListener` and vice versa, with **no static registry anywhere** — each consumer's service is its
+own object, compared by reference. `MenuListenerTest`'s `twoServiceCase_*` tests pin this for click, drag, close
+and quit independently.
+
+If your product builds its own `InventoryService` subclass (via `InventoryService.createListener` — see
+Extension points below) and registers it, it participates in the same rule automatically: as long as every menu
+your consumer builds goes through your own `InventoryService` instance, other consumers' listeners will never
+touch it, and yours will never touch theirs.
+
+## The item-return contract
+
+Any component implementing `ItemHoldingComponent` (a deposit slot, an input tray — anything holding a real player
+item across clicks) is guaranteed to get it back, on every path the basic mechanism drives a close through:
+
+| Path | Guarantee |
+|---|---|
+| A genuine user close (Escape, click outside) | `MenuListener.onClose`'s tracked branch calls `MenuTarget.returnHeldItemsOnUserClose(Player)`; `ChestMenu`'s implementation is the same guarded return every other path uses. |
+| `ChestMenu.close(player, CloseReason.RELOAD/PLUGIN_DISABLE/PLAYER_DISCONNECT/NAVIGATION/...)` | `returnHeldItems(player, reason, skipIfSlotEmpty=true)` — the `skipIfSlotEmpty` guard means a click that already moved the item out physically (Bukkit's own pickup handling, mid-flight when the close fires) is never double-issued; the held state is just cleared. |
+| A vetoed close (`MenuTarget.vetoesClose` returns `true`) followed by the player disconnecting or a reload/shutdown before the scheduled reopen tick runs | `InventoryService.recordPendingReopen`/`consumePendingReopen` (an atomic per-player map) plus `InventoryService.closeAll` draining it on top of the tracker sweep — whichever of the reopen task, `onQuit`, or the reload/shutdown sweep runs first returns the items exactly once. |
+| `MenuFlow.switchTo(String)` to a differently-sized/titled panel, or `MenuFlow.end()` | Closes the outgoing menu with `CloseReason.NAVIGATION` before discarding it — never a bare replace-and-abandon. |
+| `MenuFlow.switchTo`/`rerender()` in place (matching size+title, or `ChestMenu.adoptComponentsFrom` directly) | A dropped slot's `ItemHoldingComponent` is drained (`CloseReason.NAVIGATION`) before the slot is cleared. |
+
+**What is NOT covered:** a plain `SlotView.interactive(true)` slot that is *not* an `ItemHoldingComponent` — the
+mechanism has no way to know an interactive slot is holding something worth returning without the marker
+interface. A product that lets players freely place items in an interactive slot and expects them back on close
+must implement `ItemHoldingComponent` for that slot's component. `ChestMenu.close(Player)` (the plain, reason-less
+overload) also does NOT return held items by itself — it exists for callers that have already reasoned about
+whether a return is needed; a caller switching a player to a different menu should use
+`close(player, CloseReason.NAVIGATION)` (which is exactly what `MenuFlow.switchTo` does) rather than this overload.
+
+Full narrative and the three fix rounds that hardened this contract before E6.1 shipped: `docs/phase-e6-inventory.md` §3.
+
+## The `handleDepositClick` seam
+
+`MenuTarget` has one hook for "a click needs item-routing logic more specialised than the plain
+cancel-unless-interactive policy": `default boolean handleDepositClick(InventoryClickEvent event) { return false; }`.
+`MenuListener` calls it for both a click landing in the menu's own inventory and a shift-click originating in the
+player's inventory, BEFORE applying its own cancel/interactive/dispatch policy. Returning `true` means "I already
+did everything this click needed, including cancelling the event if required" — `MenuListener` does nothing
+further. This is where Oriel's ~130-line deposit-slot place/pickup/swap/shift-transfer logic lives entirely; the
+basic mechanism ships the seam, never the logic behind it.
+
+A product building its own item-routing feature (a trade-input slot, a fee box) implements this hook on its own
+`ChestMenu` subclass (see Extension points) rather than trying to route everything through the plain interactive
+flag, which only supports "cancelled or not" — no placeholder-swap, no partial-stack accept/reject, no
+distinguishing a top-inventory click from a bottom-inventory shift-transfer.
+
+## Extension points
+
+`ChestMenu`/`ChestMenuBuilder`/`MenuListener`/`InventoryService` are all non-final with protected hooks, so a
+product can subclass instead of re-copying the ~230-line `ChestMenu` core (this is exactly what lets Oriel's own
+rebase subclass `keystone-inventory`'s `ChestMenu` instead of forking it):
+
+| Class | Hooks a subclass overrides |
+|---|---|
+| `ChestMenu` | `renderAll`, `rerenderSlot`, `applyFillIfEmpty`, `ensureTitleFor`, `resolveTitle` (all protected, overridable); `renderOne(int, Component<?>, Player)` returns `boolean` (touched), not a package-private type; `newRenderContext`/`newSlotView` factory hooks let a subclass supply its own `RenderContext`/`SlotView` implementation while `renderOne`'s own untouched-detection (via the public `SlotView.wasTouched()`) keeps working; `dispatchOnOpen` protected; protected fields `components`/`inventory`/`holder`/`viewer`/`titleSource`/`builderInteractiveSlots`; settable `titleSource` (a plain `Supplier<String>`) for a subclass with its own animated-title mechanism; `handleDepositClick`/`vetoesClose`/`isInteractive`/`setInteractive` all overridable (inherited from `MenuTarget`). |
+| `ChestMenuBuilder` | Protected constructor (`super(service)` from a subclass builder in another package); `newMenu(...)` factory hook returns the `ChestMenu` `build()` hands back — override to return your own subclass; `applyBorder`/`applyLine` protected. Fluent methods return `ChestMenuBuilder`; a subclass builder re-declares the ones it needs a covariant return type for. |
+| `MenuListener` | Non-final; `handlePlayerInventoryClick`/`ownHolder` protected; every `@EventHandler` method public (already overridable). |
+| `InventoryService` | `createListener(JavaPlugin)` factory hook — override to return your own `MenuListener` subclass; `registerListeners` calls it instead of hard-coding `new MenuListener(...)`. |
+
+A subclass never needs package-private access to anything — `ChestClickContext`/`ChestRenderContext`/`ChestSlotView`/
+`PassthroughPlaceholders` stay package-private specifically because the extension points above are enough;
+`ExtensionPointsTest` (in a *different* package, `inventory.extensibility`) proves this by overriding `newMenu`
+and `renderOne` from outside `inventory.chest` entirely.
+
+## Panel flows and pages
+
+`MenuFlow<S extends FlowState>` drives a sequence of `ChestMenu`s sharing one typed state object — panel-by-id
+navigation (`switchTo`/`back`/`end`), `suspend()`/`resume()` for an anvil/sign detour, and (reconciled from
+Gangland's `MultiPanelInventory`) an in-place switch — no close event, no flicker — when the target panel's size
+and title both match what's already open. `PageConfig` computes the arithmetic for a paginated rectangular region
+(`PagedRegion.render(builder, config, entries, page)` places `ItemStack` + `ClickHandler` entries into it); the
+consumer builds its own previous/next buttons.
+
+## Classes
+
+| Class | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `Menu`, `MenuBuilder`, `CloseReason` | The base contracts every menu type implements/builds against. |
+| `Component`, `RenderContext`, `SlotView`, `ItemHoldingComponent` | The render-time seam: what a component writes into a slowarning: in the working copy of 'docs/phase-e6-inventory.md', LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it
t, and the marker for "holds a real player item across clicks". |
+| `ItemComponent`, `FillComponent`, `BorderComponent`, `LineComponent` | Plain `ItemStack`+handlers component and the three decoration shapes. |
+| `ClickKind`, `ClickHandler`, `ClickContext`, `SlotHandlers` | Per-slot, most-specific-first click routing. |
+| `MenuHolder`, `MenuTarget` | The Bukkit `InventoryHolder` (carries the owning `InventoryService`) and the seam every concrete menu type implements. |
+| `MenuListener` | One class: click, drag, close, quit — for every menu inventory one `InventoryService` owns. |
+| `OpenMenuTracker`, `DefaultOpenMenuTracker`, `NavigationHistory` | Who has what menu open right now, and the per-player "back" trail. |
+| `MenuRegistry`, `InMemoryMenuRegistry`, `MenuOpener` | Named menu factories, opened by name. |
+| `InventoryService` | Per-consumer root: owns the tracker, registry, and pending-vetoed-reopen bookkeeping; `BeanLifecycle`. |
+| `ChestMenu`, `ChestMenuBuilder` | The concrete chest-shaped menu and its fluent builder. |
+| `MenuFlow`, `MenuFlowBuilder`, `Panel`, `FlowState` | Multi-panel flow coordinator. |
+| `PageConfig`, `PagedRegion` | Pagination arithmetic and rectangular-region rendering. |
